# Re-route imports on refactor

When moving a symbol (constant, type, function, class) to a new module, update
every import site in this repo to the new location. Never leave a compatibility
re-export behind.

The old location is every path that made the symbol reachable: the declaring
module, **every barrel that re-exported it**, and every `package.json` subpath
whose barrel reached it. Deleting the declaring module is not enough — barrels
are what consumers actually import, so each one drops the symbol too.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
// src/base/token-types.ts — new location
export const PHANTOM_TOKEN_TYPES = [...];
// src/sdk/market/tokens.ts — old location shims the old path
export { PHANTOM_TOKEN_TYPES } from "../../base/token-types.js";
// src/sdk/router/strategies.ts — consumer never moved
import { PHANTOM_TOKEN_TYPES } from "../market/tokens.js";

// ✅ GOOD
// src/base/token-types.ts — new location
export const PHANTOM_TOKEN_TYPES = [...];
// src/sdk/market/tokens.ts — old location no longer mentions the symbol
export const getTokenType = ...;
// src/sdk/router/strategies.ts — consumer imports from the new home
import { PHANTOM_TOKEN_TYPES } from "../../base/token-types.js";
```

## A re-export that climbs out of its own directory is a red flag

A module speaks for what it holds, and a barrel for the tree below it, so every
`export ... from` specifier is `"./x.js"` or `"./sub/index.js"`. One starting
with `../` publishes something the file does not own.

## A moved symbol is public from exactly one path

A published barrel is not exempt. Curated public surface is not a reason to keep
a symbol reachable from its old entry: that barrel drops it, and consumers
import from the entry that owns its new home.
